# Art / animation comparisons are reference points, not copy requests

When this user compares visuals, mood, or “dimension” (e.g. 2D vs 3D, composition, palette) to **named films, streams, studios, or memes** (examples they have used: Disney features, “lofi study” type loops, Ghibli-adjacent mood), they mean **communication shorthand** — a **reference point** for what they have already seen in the world.

- **Do not** assume they want to **reproduce** copyrighted character designs, signature house styles shot-for-shot, or identifiable scenes.
- **Do not** assume intent to **steal art**; they are describing a **category** or **axis** (e.g. cozy illustrated 2D room scene vs crisp 3D feature look) because that is how humans point at visual space.
- **Do** translate named references into **generic art-direction language**: medium (2D illustration vs 3D-ish rendering), line, shape language, color story, lighting, camera, density, mood, audience tone — then work from that unless they explicitly ask to mirror a specific work.

If something could be read as “make it that IP,” **clarify once** in plain language; default assumption: **reference only**, Halalit-appropriate and family-friendly unless stated otherwise.
